PS5LM: a ggml scheduler reserves for the splits its graphs really have, not one split per graph node.

On the PS5 every byte a title allocates is real memory. ggml_backend_sched_new reserved a context for one split
per graph node with GGML_SCHED_MAX_SPLIT_INPUTS (30) inputs copied each, 20,160 bytes a node, plus four backend-id
arrays of 61 entries a node. llama.cpp sizes Qwen3.5's graph at max(40 x ubatch, 32 x tensors): 27,232 nodes at
-ub 256, so ~548 MiB, and the projector's 8,192 nodes another ~165 MiB. On the console the model's graph has 3,801
nodes in 2 splits with 10 inputs, the projector's 1 split.

The copies and views of a split graph now go into small contexts (tensor headers only): the first holds 256
tensors, a full one gets a next one twice its size, and ggml_backend_sched_split_graph empties them and keeps them
for the next graph. Tensors never move. The four id arrays start at the graph size and grow in split_graph to the
graph copy's size, the bound upstream already sizes the copy's node and leaf arrays with. Splits, their inputs and
graph inputs already grow on demand upstream. Any number of backends: this replaces the earlier
0011-ggml-sched-one-backend-one-split.patch, which did it for one-backend schedulers (whisper.cpp's) only.

